      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo truly understand what water is, we can look at it through a few different lenses: chemical, physical, ecological, and cultural.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a molecule made up of three atoms: **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**. This is why its famous chemical formula is **$H_2O$**.\n\n* **The Structure:** The molecule has a \"bent\" shape. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. \n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of this polarity, water molecules act like tiny magnets, attracting other polar and charged substances (like salt and sugar). This allows water to dissolve more substances than any other liquid, earning it the title of the \"universal solvent.\" This property is crucial for biological processes, as it allows nutrients to be transported through blood and plant sap.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The standard state found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n  * *A special quirk of water:* Unlike almost every other substance, water **expands and becomes less dense when it freezes**. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life, and life on Earth might never have evolved.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F). Water vapor is a major part of the Earth's atmosphere and weather system.\n\nWater also has a **high specific heat capacity**, meaning it can absorb and release large amounts of heat without a 